    \u0412 \u0445\u043e\u0434\u0435 \u0434\u043e\u043a\u0430\u0437\u0430\u0442\u0435\u043b\u044c\u0441\u0442\u0432\u0430
    \u043e\u043d \u0441\u0434\u0435\u043b\u0430\u043b \u043b\u043e\u0433\u0438\u0447\u0435\u0441\u043a\u0438\u0439
    \u0441\u043a\u0430\u0447\u043e\u043a, \u0437\u0430\u044f\u0432\u0438\u0432: \u00ab\u042d\u0442\u043e
    \u043e\u0447\u0435\u0432\u0438\u0434\u043d\u043e\u00bb. \u0425\u0430\u0440\u0434\u0438 \u043f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0441\u043f\u0440\u043e\u0441\u0438\u043b:
    \u00ab\u041e\u0447\u0435\u0432\u0438\u0434\u043d\u043e?!\u00bb. \u041d\u0430 \u0447\u0442\u043e
    \u0420\u0430\u043c\u0430\u043d\u0443\u0434\u0436\u0430\u043d \u0431\u0435\u0437 \u0442\u0435\u043d\u0438
    \u0441\u043e\u043c\u043d\u0435\u043d\u0438\u044f \u043d\u0430\u043f\u0438\u0441\u0430\u043b
    \u043e\u0442\u0432\u0435\u0442. \u041a\u0430\u043a \u0432\u044b\u044f\u0441\u043d\u0438\u043b\u043e\u0441\u044c
    \u043f\u043e\u0437\u0436\u0435, \u043e\u043d \u0438\u0441\u043f\u043e\u043b\u044c\u0437\u043e\u0432\u0430\u043b
    \u0441\u043b\u043e\u0436\u043d\u0435\u0439\u0448\u0438\u0435 \u043f\u0440\u0435\u043e\u0431\u0440\u0430\u0437\u043e\u0432\u0430\u043d\u0438\u044f,
    \u043d\u0435 \u0438\u0437\u0432\u0435\u0441\u0442\u043d\u044b\u0435 \u0437\u0430\u043f\u0430\u0434\u043d\u043e\u0439
    \u043c\u0430\u0442\u0435\u043c\u0430\u0442\u0438\u043a\u0435, \u043f\u0440\u043e\u0441\u0442\u043e
    \u00ab\u043f\u043e\u0442\u043e\u043c\u0443 \u0447\u0442\u043e \u0442\u0430\u043a \u0447\u0443\u0432\u0441\u0442\u0432\u043e\u0432\u0430\u043b\u00bb.\n\n\U0001F48E
    10. \u041c\u0438\u0441\u0442\u0438\u0447\u0435\u0441\u043a\u043e\u0435 \u0441\u043e\u0432\u043f\u0430\u0434\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0435
    \u0434\u0430\u0442 \u0420\u0430\u043c\u0430\u043d\u0443\u0434\u0436\u0430\u043d \u0440\u043e\u0434\u0438\u043b\u0441\u044f
    22 \u0434\u0435\u043a\u0430\u0431\u0440\u044f 1887 \u0433\u043e\u0434\u0430. \u0423\u043c\u0435\u0440
    \u043e\u043d 26 \u0430\u043f\u0440\u0435\u043b\u044f 1920 \u0433\u043e\u0434\u0430. \u0418\u043d\u0442\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0441\u043d\u043e,
    \u0447\u0442\u043e \u0437\u043d\u0430\u043c\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0442\u044b\u0439 \u043d\u0435\u043c\u0435\u0446\u043a\u0438\u0439
    \u043c\u0430\u0442\u0435\u043c\u0430\u0442\u0438\u043a \u0414\u0430\u0432\u0438\u0434 \u0413\u0438\u043b\u044c\u0431\u0435\u0440\u0442,
    \u0447\u044c\u0438 \u0440\u0430\u0431\u043e\u0442\u044b \u0432\u043e \u043c\u043d\u043e\u0433\u043e\u043c
    \u043f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u043a\u043b\u0438\u043a\u0430\u044e\u0442\u0441\u044f \u0441 \u0442\u0435\u043c\u0430\u043c\u0438
    \u0420\u0430\u043c\u0430\u043d\u0443\u0434\u0436\u0430\u043d\u0430, \u0440\u043e\u0434\u0438\u043b\u0441\u044f
    \u0432 \u043e\u0434\u0438\u043d \u0434\u0435\u043d\u044c (22 \u0434\u0435\u043a\u0430\u0431\u0440\u044f),
    \u043d\u043e \u043d\u0430 25 \u043b\u0435\u0442 \u0440\u0430\u043d\u044c\u0448\u0435 (1862).
    \u0410 \u0435\u0433\u043e \u043a\u0443\u043c\u0438\u0440 \u0438 \u0431\u0443\u0434\u0443\u0449\u0438\u0439
    \u043d\u0430\u0441\u0442\u0430\u0432\u043d\u0438\u043a \u0425\u0430\u0440\u0434\u0438 \u0431\u044b\u043b
    \u0441\u0442\u0430\u0440\u0448\u0435 \u0420\u0430\u043c\u0430\u043d\u0443\u0434\u0436\u0430\u043d\u0430
    \u0440\u043e\u0432\u043d\u043e \u043d\u0430 10 \u043b\u0435\u0442."
  interestingFactsEn: "Ramanujan's life and creativity are full of amazing, and sometimes mystical, stories that make his image not just genius but almost legendary, and these are the most interesting and fascinating facts, the chickens and the details of his biography.\n\n1. The number 1729: The taxi, which became legendary, is probably the most famous math chicken in history. One day in 1918, H. H. Hardy visited the patient Ramanujan in the sanatorium. Hardy started talking with a complaint: > \"I came by a taxi with a very boring number of 1729. I hope that's not a bad sign.\"And Ramanujan, not thinking for a second, replied: > No, it's a very interesting number! It's the least natural number that you can imagine as the sum of two cubes in two different ways.\" Indeed, 1729 = 13 + 123 = 93 + 103 (1 + 1728 = 729 + 1000). Today, this number bears the name Hardy-Ramanujan.\n\nHe said, \"My Lord, I have been sent to you by God.\"This feature was striking and at the same time perplexed by his strict and atheistic mentor Hardy.\n\n3. The difficulties with food in England, being an orthodox brahman, Ramanujan was a strict vegetarian. In London, during World War I (1914–1918), it was incredibly difficult to find adequate food. Because of his lack of food and his inability to cook on his own, he often ate one rice and boiled vegetables without specialties.Hardy, who wasn't cooking himself, sometimes desperately tried to find food for him, which was a difficult task in wartime.\n\n4. At the age of 16, he found Carr's book \"The Collection of Basic Results... \" The most smoking book was full of errors and typologies (in some formulas there were no coefficients), but Ramanujan, without other sources, proved each formula on his own. If he found a mistake in the book, he did not throw away the formula, but tried to find the right option by learning in other people's missedes.It turned him into a \"human machine\" to test the truth of the theorem in his mind.\n\n5 Hardy's letter: \"It could be a mystification.\" In 1913, Hardy, after receiving a letter from an unknown Indian clerk, first thought it was a prank. He and Littlewood spent several hours trying to figure out whether their author had created an algorithm or if it was a genius. Later, Hardy said that the formulas from that letter simply couldn't be made up because \"they beat their eyes with their beauty and surprise.\"Only those with extraordinary talents could prove it.\n\n6. The suicide attempt at the youth station, when Ramanujan lived in extreme poverty and his mathematical work was not recognized, he was so desperate that he decided to kill himself one day by throwing himself under a train at Madrasa station, when he heard someone screaming to him that the passengers were late for the train and he had left the tracks.Ramanujan took it as a sign of fate and later said that it was at that moment that he had promised himself not to give up.\n\n7. \"Lost notebook\" was found 56 years after his death in 1920, it was thought that he had left only three notebooks. However, in 1976, a mathematician named George Andrews found another notebook in Cambridge's archives, which no one had opened since 1920. It contained about 600 formulas, most of which were completely new. This notebook was called \"lost\", and its contents are still not fully deciphered.\n\n8. Time advance: \"Fake Theta Features\", in his last letter to Hardy a few months before his death (1920), Ramanujan described a group of functions that he called \"fake Theta Functions\". The mathematicians for half a century did not understand what it was. Only at the beginning of the twenty-first century did it become apparent that these functions are part of modern mathematical physics and are used in string and quantum gravity theory.Ramanujan simply \"saw\" them 70 years earlier than official science.\n\n9. He could not prove it strictly, but... a funny paradox: when Hardy asked Ramanujan to bring hard evidence to one of his theories, he began writing in a notebook, and he made a logical leap in the evidence, saying, \"It's obvious.\" Hardy re-explained, \"Apparently?\" What did Ramanujan write the answer without a shadow of doubt.Later, it appeared that he had used complex changes, not known to western mathematics, simply \"because he felt that way\".\n\n10. The mysterious coincidence of the dates of Ramanujan was born on December 22, 1887, and died on April 26, 1920, and it is interesting that the famous German mathematician David Gilbert, whose works largely overlap with those of Ramanujan, was born on the same day (22 December), but 25 years earlier (1862), and his idol and future mentor Hardy were exactly 10 years older than Ramanujan."
